UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 3: Food safety and hygiene', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food. Tie hair back, wear clean clothes or an apron, and cover a cut with a clean waterproof dressing. If you are vomiting or have diarrhoea, you stay out of food preparation until you have recovered. That is protection for other people, not a punishment.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose the safer setup: (a) cooking while sick with a stomach bug because the soup will boil, or (b) asking an adult to cook and staying away from the food.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChoice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen are still at risk.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked. The cook can contaminate benches, taps and food that is not cooked.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDesign a short hygiene checklist with four items for a home cook. Include one rule about illness. Use different examples from the dough scene.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Food Technology","learning_intention":"Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food. Tie hair back, wear clean clothes or an apron, and cover a cut with a clean waterproof dressing. If you are vomiting or have diarrhoea, you stay out of food preparation until you have recovered. That is protection for other people, not a punishment.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["FT5-SAF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Design a short hygiene checklist with four items for a home cook. Include one rule about illness. Use different examples from the dough scene. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using hygiene on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked.. Correction: Choice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked. The cook can contaminate benches, taps and food that is not cooked. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating hygiene on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with hygiene, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about hygiene with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for hygiene. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for hygiene. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work","Choice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen","Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result"],"question":"Choose the safer setup: (a) cooking while sick with a stomach bug because the soup will boil, or (b) asking an adult to cook and staying away from the food. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for hygiene; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen","Careful use of hair with clear evidence","Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named hair, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Name hair only, without linking it to hygiene","Apply hygiene to new material and name the hair used"],"question":"Which response best applies hygiene with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on hygiene. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Choice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves hygiene","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough","Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Design a short hygiene checklist with four items for a home cook","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked","Name hair only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies hygiene to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about hygiene with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating hygiene on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using hygiene on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for hygiene. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with hygiene, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked.. Correction: Choice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-food-technology-y9-w02-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 alignment. Food Technology 7–10 (2025) says Stage 4 teachers may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (FT4-ADJ-01). This lesson uses FT5-SAF-01 as a pending proposal. The 2019 syllabus remains in teach-out. Not NESA verified. Practical cooking is optional and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise hygiene.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Food Technology","success_criteria":["I can use hygiene with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify hair and explain why it matters.","I can practise illness independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cover needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough. Safer choice: tie the hair, wash hands, cover the cut, then knead. If they had vomited that morning, they do not knead; someone else does.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose the safer setup: (a) cooking while sick with a stomach bug because the soup will boil, or (b) asking an adult to cook and staying away from the food.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChoice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen are still at risk.","explain":"Personal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food. Tie hair back, wear clean clothes or an apron, and cover a cut with a clean waterproof dressing. If you are vomiting or have diarrhoea, you stay out of food preparation until you have recovered. That is protection for other people, not a punishment.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked. The cook can contaminate benches, taps and food that is not cooked. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Tie hair and cover a cut before kneading dough.","caption":"Personal hygiene is a set of visible controls.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unsafe setup</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">loose hair, open cut</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safer setup</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tied hair, dressing</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough. Safer choice: tie the hair, wash h Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough. Safer choice: tie the hair, wash h Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough. Safer choice: tie the hair, wash hands, cover the cut, then knead. If they had vomited that morning, they do not knead; someone else does. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A person who is vomiting does not prepare food.","caption":"Cooking later does not cancel illness while handling.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Ill today</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">vomiting</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stay away</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">someone else cooks</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked. The cook can contaminate benches, taps and food that is not cooked. Here is a more productive model: A student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough. Safer choice: tie the hair, wash hands, cover the cut, then knead. If they had vomited that morning, they do not knead; someone else does.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChoice (b). Boiling the soup later does not undo handling while infectious, and other ready-to-eat foods in the kitchen are still at risk.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Believing that any illness is fine if the food will be cooked. The cook can contaminate benches, taps and food that is not cooked. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about hygiene?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPersonal hygiene keeps hair, dirty clothes, uncovered cuts and illness away from food. Tie hair back, wear clean clothes or an apron, and cover a cut with a clean waterproof dressing. If you are vomiting or have diarrhoea, you stay out of food preparation until you have recovered. That is protection for other people, not a punishment. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 3: Food safety and hygiene","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Food safety and hygiene","week":2,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA student with a loose fringe and an uncovered cut on the thumb wants to knead dough. Safer choice: tie the hair, wash hands, cover the cut, then knead. If they had vomited that morning, they do not knead; someone else does.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Tie hair and cover a cut before kneading dough.","caption":"Personal hygiene is a set of visible controls.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unsafe setup</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">loose hair, open cut</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Safer setup</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tied hair, dressing</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A person who is vomiting does not prepare 